<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="myricetin gentiobioside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7140" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME:MYRICETIN Myricetin| and its glycosides belong to the subclass of flavonols, which constitute one of the largest groups within the flavonoids and stand out because of the magnitude of modifications on the flavonol core |CITS:[17587669]| |CITS:[16137727]|. Glucosylation is an important and regular modification reaction in plant metabolism. The attachment of sugar molecules enables several strategies; it is protective, stabilizing and adds properties such as solubility for transport of molecules tolerance |CITS: [10973093]|. Flavonols inclusive |FRAME:MYRICETIN| and its glucosides are wide spread in the plant kingdom (e.g. |CITS:[22279376]|) and have a variety of useful pharmacological properties. |FRAME:MYRICETIN Myricetin| prevents aggregation of amyloids which cause Alzheimer&apos;s disease |CITS:[22961751]| |CITS:[22995388]| and show antiviral, antibacterial, antiprotozoal, hypoglycemic, anticancer and diuretic activity |CITS:[22279376]| |CITS:[22195992]|. Flavonol glucosides, inclusive |FRAME:MYRICETIN| glucosides, are involved in flower coloration |CITS:[22485167]|, hence playing a part in developmental processes in plants. Myricetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyltransferases |CITS:[Fukuchi-Mitzutani11]| |CITS:[19733370]| and myricetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside 1,6-glucosyltransferases |CITS:[19561332]| |CITS:[17940060]| are the enzymes catalyzing the formation of |FRAME:CPD-14844| via |FRAME:CPD-14843|. Although the glucosyltransferases serve a broader spectrum of flavonoids and phenolics (for example see |FRAME:PWY-7137|, |FRAME:PWY-7143|) the observed strict regiospecificity of the glucosyltransferases towards their potential substrates emphasizes a strong preference of flavonoles over other flavonoids or phenolics. The occurrence and physiological role of the various flavonol gentiobiosides remains to be shown.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="hawkins" />
    <Author name="O-28" />
    <Author name="hartmut" />
  </Authors>
